nces provide 4x the capacity of t3.micro, reducing the instance count needed and improving cost efficiency per request. ElastiCache offloads 60-80% of read queries from RDS, reducing database CPU and enabling the db.t3.medium instance to handle remaining load comfortably. CloudFront caching (50% hit rate per N4) reduces origin load and improves global latency. The original architecture's undersized instances would thrash under load, degrading user experience. The proposed architecture right-sizes compute and adds a purpose-built caching layer.\n\n**Cost Optimization**: Original monthly cost is $236.13. Proposed cost is $687.58, within the $750 budget (N5). Key optimizations: t3.medium provides better price-performance than over-provisioning with t3.small (which would require 5-6 instances at peak); ElastiCache ($99.28/month) prevents costly database scaling; CloudFront caching reduces compute requirements; scaling to 4 instances for 60 peak hours ($14.98) rather than running excess capacity continuously. The original architecture would fail at peak load, requiring emergency scaling or re-architecture. Reserved Instances for baseline capacity (2x t3.medium, db.t3.medium, ElastiCache) would reduce steady-state costs 30-40% after the first year.\n\n**Sustainability**: The proposed architecture minimizes waste through demand-based scaling, running only required capacity. t3.medium instances run at higher utilization (70% target) than the original architecture's t3.micro instances under variable load, improving energy efficiency per request served. ElastiCache reduces redundant database queries, lowering computational overhead. CloudFront edge caching reduces data transfer over long-haul networks. The architecture follows AWS's shared responsibility model for carbon-efficient infrastructure while optimizing application-layer resource consumption.",
